{"rows":[{"n":1,"file":"topics/01-java-core-jvm.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":2,"file":"topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":3,"file":"topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":4,"file":"topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":5,"file":"topics/05-db-core-index-lock.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":6,"file":"topics/06-db-scaling.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":7,"file":"topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":8,"file":"topics/08-message-queue.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":9,"file":"topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":12,"file":"topics/12-architecture-patterns.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":13,"file":"topics/13-security-oauth2.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":14,"file":"topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":15,"file":"topics/15-network-i-o-models.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":16,"file":"topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.json","topic_type":"algorithm","system_design_items":["16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1"]},{"n":17,"file":"topics/17-rest-api-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":18,"file":"topics/18-query-optimization.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":19,"file":"topics/19-dsa-leetcode.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":20,"file":"topics/20-observability-sre.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":21,"file":"topics/21-linux-production-debug.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":22,"file":"topics/22-low-level-design-ood.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":23,"file":"topics/23-java-concurrency-coding.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":24,"file":"topics/24-domain-driven-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":25,"topic_type":"microservice","file":"topics/25-microservice.json"},{"n":26,"file":"topics/26-testing-strategy.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":28,"file":"topics/28-distributed-lock-lease.json","topic_type":"design"}],"row":{"n":9,"file":"topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech.json","topic_type":"design"},"meta":{"topic_type":"design","key":"09-distributed-tx-fintech","vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)","title":"Transaction giữa các service (fintech)","intro":"Phần JD nhấn mạnh nhất: 'integration between systems'. Đảm bảo correctness khi tiền đi qua nhiều service mà không có transaction toàn cục — nơi phân biệt senior thật trong fintech.","tags":["Saga","2PC","Outbox","TCC","Idempotency","Reconciliation","Double-entry","BigDecimal"]},"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)","title":"Transactions across services (fintech)","intro":"What the job description leans on hardest: integration between systems. Keeping money correct as it crosses several services with no global transaction — where real seniority shows in fintech.","tags":["Saga","2PC","Outbox","TCC","Idempotency","Reconciliation","Double-entry","BigDecimal"]},"created_at":"2026-07-31","updated_at":"2026-10-25"},"topicMeta":{"1":{"en":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"},"vi":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"}},"2":{"en":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"},"vi":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"}},"3":{"en":{"label":"Spring Boot deep dive + build"},"vi":{"label":"Spring Boot deep + build"}},"4":{"en":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"},"vi":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"}},"5":{"en":{"label":"DB core: indexes & locking"},"vi":{"label":"DB core: index & lock"}},"6":{"en":{"label":"DB scaling"},"vi":{"label":"DB scaling"}},"7":{"en":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"},"vi":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"}},"8":{"en":{"label":"Message queue"},"vi":{"label":"Message queue"}},"9":{"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"}},"10":{"en":{"label":"System design + rate limit"},"vi":{"label":"System design + rate limit"}},"11":{"en":{"label":"System design cases"},"vi":{"label":"System design cases"}},"12":{"en":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"},"vi":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"}},"13":{"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"},"vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"}},"14":{"en":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"},"vi":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"}},"15":{"en":{"label":"Network & I/O models"},"vi":{"label":"Network & I/O models"}},"16":{"en":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"},"vi":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"}},"17":{"en":{"label":"REST API design"},"vi":{"label":"Thiết kế REST API"}},"18":{"en":{"label":"Query optimisation"},"vi":{"label":"Tối ưu query"}},"19":{"en":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"},"vi":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"}},"20":{"en":{"label":"Observability & SRE"},"vi":{"label":"Observability & SRE"}},"21":{"en":{"label":"Linux & production debug"},"vi":{"label":"Linux & production debug"}},"22":{"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"},"vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"}},"23":{"en":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"},"vi":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"}},"24":{"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"},"vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"}},"25":{"en":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"},"vi":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"}},"26":{"en":{"label":"Testing"},"vi":{"label":"Testing"}},"27":{"en":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"},"vi":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"}},"28":{"en":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"}}},"reviewedAt":{"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q1":"2026-09-12","09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2":"2026-08-19","09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3":"2026-08-19","09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q4":"2026-09-12","09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q5":"2026-08-19","09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q6":"2026-08-19","09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1":"2026-08-23","09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2":"2026-08-23","09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3":"2026-08-19","09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q4":"2026-09-12","09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q5":"2026-08-19","09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6":"2026-09-12"},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"Distributed workflow correctness","items":[{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q1","q":"How should you choose local ACID, 2PC/XA, or a Saga?","a":"Start with the invariant, not the pattern name.\n\n1. [[g:Local ACID first]]: keep data that must change atomically in one authority and one database transaction.\n2. [[b:2PC/XA]]: use it only when every participant, coordinator, latency budget, and recovery owner are deliberate and supported.\n3. [[b:Saga]]: use local transactions plus explicit forward recovery or compensation when the boundary is genuinely distributed.\n\n2PC is not universally wrong. While prepared and unresolved, participants can hold locks or other resources; a coordinator or participant failure leaves an in-doubt recovery problem. That makes it a bounded option, not a good default for long human or provider calls.\n\n:::tip Bottom line\nA service boundary alone is not a reason to distribute a transaction. Name the authority, failure budget, recovery owner, and user-visible states before choosing the pattern (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q5).\n:::\n\n:::deep\n2PC can provide atomic commit within its protocol scope, but not unlimited availability.\n\nA Saga does not provide atomic intermediate states; it coordinates local commits toward a declared desired state under eventual consistency. Each local transaction remains atomic only within its own authority.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n**What would change this answer** — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it:\n\n- Which provider contract the example assumes — Stripe and Adyen differ on idempotency keys and retries — or say the pseudocode is provider-neutral.\n- The real reconciliation SLA for payment, inventory and booking. Without it, \"eventual\" is a hope, not an operational contract.\n- Whether 2PC is actually available in the target databases and drivers and has a recovery owner; if not, 2PC stays a bounded comparison, never runnable guidance.\n- The retention window for late retries and callbacks. If it outlives the idempotency-key TTL, the recommendation is falsified until a durable business reference exists.\n- Whether the ledger can prove a zero-sum posting invariant under crash and replay. One failing property test falsifies any \"exactly-once money movement\" wording.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2","q":"Saga: choreography vs orchestration? Compensating transaction?","a":"<figure class='dgm'><svg viewBox='0 0 680 150' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='IBM Plex Mono, monospace' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>\n<text x='170' y='20' fill='#085239' font-weight='600'>ORCHESTRATION</text><rect x='105' y='34' width='130' height='30' rx='6' fill='#E7EAF6' stroke='#3D4E8F'/><text x='170' y='54' fill='#3D4E8F'>workflow state</text><g stroke='#3D4E8F' stroke-width='1.4'><line x1='170' y1='64' x2='75' y2='105'/><line x1='170' y1='64' x2='170' y2='105'/><line x1='170' y1='64' x2='265' y2='105'/></g><text x='170' y='135' fill='#42536C'>explicit steps, retry, compensation</text><line x1='350' y1='12' x2='350' y2='140' stroke='#C6CFDC' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/><text x='515' y='20' fill='#7d2f24' font-weight='600'>CHOREOGRAPHY</text><text x='515' y='70' fill='#42536C'>local tx &#8594; event &#8594; local tx</text><text x='515' y='105' fill='#AE4536'>loose coupling, hidden flow</text></svg></figure>\n\n[[g:Saga]] splits a long business operation into local transactions. It coordinates toward a declared desired state; a failed step uses a [[g:compensating transaction]], which is a new business operation, not a database rollback.\n\nCompensation can fail, be delayed, be rejected, or be impossible after an irreversible effect.\n\n- [[b:Choreography]]: services react to events without a conductor; useful for small, stable flows, but ownership and recovery can become hard to see.\n- [[b:Orchestration]]: a durable workflow state store drives steps, timers, retries, and compensation; it improves visibility but adds a control-plane dependency. A replicated workflow engine changes the crash mode, not the business invariant.\n\nEvery step and compensation needs an idempotent command and explicit states such as `PENDING`, `COMPENSATING`, and `RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED`. Put irreversible work after a declared pivot when possible.\n\nGeneric Saga trade-offs are cross-referenced in (25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q3); the OTA flow belongs in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5).\n\n:::warn Boundary\nIntermediate states are observable. Do not let a user or another workflow spend against a balance, hold, or payment that has not reached the authority's settled state.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3","q":"The Outbox pattern, and why is dual-write dangerous?","a":"<figure class='dgm'><svg viewBox='0 0 680 145' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='IBM Plex Mono, monospace' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'><rect x='35' y='35' width='190' height='75' rx='8' fill='#E4F1EB' stroke='#0B6E4F'/><text x='130' y='27' fill='#085239' font-weight='600'>ONE LOCAL TX</text><text x='130' y='65' fill='#14233A'>state + outbox intent</text><text x='350' y='72' fill='#42536C'>&#8594; relay / CDC &#8594;</text><rect x='480' y='35' width='155' height='75' rx='8' fill='#F6E8E5' stroke='#AE4536'/><text x='557' y='65' fill='#7d2f24'>consumer</text><text x='557' y='84' fill='#AE4536'>deduplicate</text><text x='340' y='135' fill='#42536C'>at-least-once publish; no end-to-end exactly-once</text></svg></figure>\n\n[[r:Dual write]] means committing business state and publishing externally as separate steps. A failure between them creates drift. [[g:Transactional Outbox]] writes the integration event intent in the same local transaction as the business change.\n\nThe outbox closes only the [[g:state &#8596; publish-intent]] boundary. A relay or CDC connector may publish at least once, and can crash after publish before marking progress.\n\nThe consumer therefore needs an Inbox, a unique event ID, or an equivalent idempotent effect in the same local transaction as its state change. Outbox does not prove that a broker delivered, a consumer applied, or a PSP settled the effect.\n\n:::tip Boundary\nThis unit owns the workflow meaning and recovery boundary.\n\nBroker confirms, acknowledgements, partitions, and replay mechanics belong to (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q2), (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6), and (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7).\n:::\n\n:::deep\nRetain an event ID, relay error/age metrics, repair and redrive policy, and a versioned integration contract. Put public integration events in the outbox, not every internal domain event (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3).\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q4","q":"How does TCC (Try-Confirm-Cancel) differ from Saga?","a":"TCC is a protocol shape, not an automatic synonym for a Saga or a seat hold.\n\n- [[b:Try]] reserves a resource.\n- [[b:Confirm]] commits the reservation.\n- [[b:Cancel]] releases it when the workflow cannot continue.\n\n<table class='cmp'><thead><tr><th></th><th>Saga</th><th>TCC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Order</td><td>perform, then compensate</td><td>reserve, then confirm or cancel</td></tr><tr><td>Visibility</td><td>intermediate business state is observable</td><td>reservation is observable and may expire</td></tr><tr><td>Isolation</td><td>depends on compensation and authority</td><td>can be stronger for the reserved resource</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>local op plus compensation</td><td>three idempotent phases per participant</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\nEach phase needs an owner, idempotency, empty-rollback handling, expiry, and recovery policy. Try, Confirm, and Cancel must each tolerate duplicate or late calls under that contract.\n\nA seat hold or PSP authorize/capture flow may be [[o:TCC-like]] only when its provider contract actually exposes those phases; do not infer it from similar names. The concrete OTA race and hold invariant are in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2).\n\n:::deep\nTCC can reduce exposure of an uncommitted resource, but it does not remove timeouts, stale reservations, provider failure, or reconciliation.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q5","q":"Two services need to stay consistent without a distributed transaction — what are the options?","a":"Choose the authority and atomic boundary first:\n\n- [[g:Local ACID]] when the invariant is genuinely shared: keep both ledger legs or the inventory transition in one bounded context and database.\n- [[b:Provider atomic contract or 2PC/XA]] only when its participant, latency, availability, and recovery contract is explicit and acceptable.\n- [[b:Saga + Outbox]] when the split is real: accept observable intermediate states, idempotent steps, compensation, and reconciliation.\n- [[b:Eventual projections]] for search, notifications, and reporting; revalidate against the authority before a commit decision.\n\nIdempotency belongs at each command, consumer, and provider boundary; these are different keys and contracts. Reconciliation is a control loop, not proof that the online invariant was safe.\n\n:::tip Key takeaway\nService boundaries should usually follow consistency boundaries, but participant count alone is not the decision rule. Ownership, reversibility, risk, latency, and recovery evidence matter.\n\nThe end-to-end money-transfer comparison lives in (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q4).\n:::\n\n:::deep\nIf a booking, payment, or ledger operation crosses a real external authority, model `PENDING`, `UNKNOWN`, `COMPENSATING`, and `RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED` rather than pretending a local rollback can undo it.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q6","q":"Distributed lock (Redis/Redlock) — when to use it, and what are the risks?","a":"A lock coordinates ownership of an attempt; it is not the authority for money or inventory correctness. A short lease can prevent duplicate scheduler work: `SET key value NX PX ttl`.\n\n[[r:Risk]]: a pause, partition, or slow operation can outlive the TTL, so an old worker and a new worker may overlap. Use an ownership token, safe release, renewal bounds, and [[g:fencing token]]s when the resource can reject stale writers.\n\n:::warn Fintech\nLet the database or provider authority enforce the invariant with a unique constraint, version, conditional write, or documented idempotency contract. A lock only reduces contention.\n\nSee (28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q1) and (28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q3).\n:::\n\n:::deep\nRedlock and other multi-node locks have implementation- and timing-dependent safety claims. Do not turn a lock expiry into a second charge, ledger posting, or event; use durable state and reconciliation instead.\n:::","difficulty":"core"}]},{"title":"Money correctness","items":[{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1","q":"Real-world scenario: a call to a PSP/bank times out and you don't know whether the money was already deducted?","a":"[[r:Unknown outcome]] means there is not enough evidence to call the operation either failed or succeeded.\n\n- Claim one local payment attempt and stable merchant reference. Use a provider idempotency key when that provider documents its scope and retention.\n- On timeout, store `PENDING` or `UNKNOWN`; do not create a new logical charge with a new key.\n- Use the provider's status inquiry or verified callback when available. If evidence remains incomplete, keep an owned unknown queue and reconcile it against provider records or settlement.\n- Move to `SUCCEEDED`, `FAILED`, `COMPENSATING`, or `RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED` only through legal, audited state transitions.\n\n:::tip Bottom line\nA timeout is a missing observation, not proof of failure. The local state machine, provider contract, and reconciliation SLA decide when the user can be told the outcome.\n:::\n\n<pre><code>CREATE TABLE payment_attempt(\n  id uuid PRIMARY KEY,\n  idempotency_key text UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n  provider_ref text UNIQUE,\n  status text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN\n    ('CREATED','SUBMITTED','PENDING','UNKNOWN','SUCCEEDED','FAILED',\n     'RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED'))\n);\n-- claim one local attempt, then call the provider outside this DB tx\nUPDATE payment_attempt SET status='SUBMITTED'\n WHERE id=:id AND status='CREATED';\nCOMMIT;\n-- timeout: preserve uncertainty, do not mark FAILED\nUPDATE payment_attempt SET status='UNKNOWN'\n WHERE id=:id AND status='SUBMITTED';\n-- inquiry/callback/reconciliation applies one legal transition\nUPDATE payment_attempt SET status='SUCCEEDED', provider_ref=:ref\n WHERE id=:id AND status IN ('PENDING','UNKNOWN','SUBMITTED');</code></pre>\n\nThe provider contract is integration-specific; not every PSP or bank offers equivalent idempotency, inquiry, callback, or retention semantics (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1).\n\nMake the unknown queue operational by storing `unknown_since`, `next_inquiry_at`, `reconciliation_deadline`, `owner`, and a risk tier.\n\n**Age/risk buckets, not a magic timeout.** Tie each bucket to the provider's response and settlement SLAs:\n- within the documented window: back off and inquire\n- past it: escalate to settlement or manual review when policy requires\n\nUntil an authority proves the outcome, client expiry does not authorize a second charge.\n\n:::deep\nTrack unknown age, retry owner, callback verification, inquiry attempts, settlement drift, and manual escalation. Auto-void or refund is a policy after evidence and eligibility, not a blind timeout handler.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2","q":"Idempotency in payments: how do you implement it airtight?","a":"Treat idempotency as three separate boundaries:\n\n1. [[g:Command claim]]: scope the key to tenant, account, operation, or endpoint; store a request fingerprint and reject the same key with a different payload.\n2. [[g:Local result]]: commit the claim, business transition, final result, and any Outbox row in one local transaction. Outbox is an additional intent row, not a replacement for the key/result record.\n3. [[g:Provider command]]: reuse the provider key or merchant reference only under that provider's documented contract.\n\n<pre><code><span class='k'>INSERT INTO</span> idempotency_keys(scope, key, request_fingerprint, status, response)\n<span class='k'>VALUES</span>(?, ?, ?, <span class='s'>'IN_PROGRESS'</span>, <span class='k'>NULL</span>);  <span class='c'>-- UNIQUE(scope, key)</span></code></pre>\n\nA duplicate with the same fingerprint returns the stored result, polls an `IN_PROGRESS` attempt, or receives a documented conflict. A different fingerprint is a conflict, not a retry.\n\nKeep records for longer than the client/provider retry and callback windows, or retain a durable business reference; a short cache TTL is not a correctness boundary.\n\n**Before production, pin the provider's behavior:**\n- API/version and account/region\n- key scope and retention\n- payload-mismatch and concurrent-reuse behavior\n- status inquiry\n- webhook authenticity/replay behavior\n- settlement/reversal semantics\n\nStripe and Adyen document different idempotency details; copy neither as a universal default.\n\n:::deep\nDo not promise that an HTTP method, broker delivery mode, or local key alone prevents an external duplicate. Name the boundary and test concurrent duplicates, late callbacks, result replay, payload mismatch, and retention expiry (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1).\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3","q":"Double-entry bookkeeping — why is it the gold standard for a ledger?","a":"A posting is a controlled batch of signed entries: for one currency or asset, the entries must balance according to the ledger's invariant. A normal transfer has debit and credit legs; issuance, fees, FX, and reversals use explicit accounts and policies rather than silently breaking the equation.\n\nThe ledger is [[g:append-only]]: do not destructively update or delete a posted entry. Correct a mistake with an authorized reversing or adjustment entry, preserving actor, reason, time, and source reference.\n\n- Commit both legs and the posting identity atomically in the ledger authority, or leave the posting pending.\n- Make the posting command idempotent with a unique business key.\n- Derive a balance from entries, or update a materialized balance in the same local transaction and reconcile it periodically.\n- Check the zero-sum invariant per posting batch and currency; it is not a single sum across unrelated currencies.\n\n:::tip Bottom line\nThe ledger is the book of record; workflow state, locks, caches, and projections must not silently become a second money authority (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q4).\n:::\n\n:::deep\nSeparate pending, posted, reversed, and disputed states. Restrict administrative adjustments, keep an immutable audit trail, and reconcile ledger postings against provider or settlement evidence.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q4","q":"Why not use float for money? What's the correct way to store it?","a":"Binary float/double cannot represent many decimal fractions exactly, so repeated arithmetic can drift.\n\nUse a representation with an explicit domain policy:\n\n- `DECIMAL(p,s)` or `BigDecimal` for decimal amounts, with scale and rounding chosen deliberately.\n- Integer minor units only when the currency and operation have a fixed, validated exponent; do not assume every currency is cents-based.\n- Store the currency code, scale, and, for FX, the rate/source/time and rounding result. Never add unlike currencies.\n- Validate currency metadata and business rounding rules against the chosen ISO 4217/domain source; `HALF_EVEN` is an example policy, not a universal law.\n\nThe representation does not prove provider settlement, chargeback, tax, KYC/AML, PCI, or other legal/compliance obligations. Keep those as integration-specific contracts and owners rather than inferring them from `DECIMAL` or `BigDecimal`.\n\n<pre><code><span class='k'>new</span> BigDecimal(&amp;quot;0.1&amp;quot;)\n    .add(<span class='k'>new</span> BigDecimal(&amp;quot;0.2&amp;quot;))\n    .setScale(<span class='n'>2</span>, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN); <span class='c'>// example: 0.30</span></code></pre>\n\n:::deep\nTest tax, fee, FX, split-payment, reversal, and reconciliation rounding at the currency boundary. The storage type prevents one class of error; it does not choose the accounting policy for you.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q5","q":"How do you prevent double-spend / balance races under high load?","a":"Do not read a balance, decide in application code, and write later. Put the linearization point in the authoritative database transaction:\n\n- Use a conditional update such as `UPDATE account SET available_minor=available_minor-? WHERE id=? AND currency=? AND available_minor>=?`; zero rows means the condition was not met.\n- Use optimistic version checks with bounded retry, or a short pessimistic row lock when contention and isolation require it.\n- Use unique constraints for one logical debit or posting, and append both ledger legs atomically.\n- Revalidate any stale projection at the final authorization boundary. A distributed lock may reduce contention, but it cannot replace these guards (28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q1).\n\n:::tip Bottom line\nMoney correctness must be anchored to the ledger/account authority: conditional state transition, uniqueness, balance invariant, and durable idempotency. Queueing or sharding can protect throughput, but they are not proof of correctness.\n:::\n\n:::deep\nFor a hot resource, serialize or partition attempts only as a capacity tactic. If a worker loses ownership or a request times out, preserve `UNKNOWN` and reconcile; never retry by creating a second logical debit.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6","q":"Eventual consistency in fintech: where is it acceptable, and where is it forbidden?","a":"Eventual consistency is acceptable for a projection whose staleness is explicit and cannot authorize money movement: displayed history, notifications, reports, analytics, and reward views.\n\nAt the final decision boundary, use the authoritative transaction or conditional write for available balance, limits, ledger posting, anti-double-spend, and inventory or hold confirmation. A read may begin the request from a projection, but it must be revalidated before commit.\n\n:::warn Do not turn a freshness preference into a universal slogan\nThe requirement is strong correctness at the invariant boundary, not that every read in a fintech product must synchronously query one database. Define freshness, `PENDING`, `UNKNOWN`, and escalation behaviour for each projection.\n:::\n\n:::deep\nFor OTA or commerce, search availability may be stale while final hold/payment revalidation is authoritative (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4). For money, reconcile ledger, provider, Outbox/relay, and projection drift with an owner and an age SLA.\n\nObserve unknown/pending age, reconciliation lag, duplicate/replay rate, provider status drift, ledger imbalance, and projection freshness.\n\nTest concurrent retries, late callbacks, provider timeout, replay, reconciliation repair, and the boundary that prevents a stale projection from authorizing money.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"09-distributed-tx-fintech","sourceOwners":{"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q4":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q5":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q6":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q1":"surviving-high-loa